From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Cormier Roussel <chadcormierroussel@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 08:01:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] airconv: A pixel shader's 1/w of SV_Position is a reciprocal,
 not an IEEE divide.

Metal's [[position]].w is 1/w; airconv divides once per pixel to give D3D's w back. On the DXIL path the builder has no
fast-math flags, so the divide compiled as a full IEEE division in every base-pass pixel shader reading SV_Position.
D3D asks no exact division of an interpolated w, and Metal Shader Converter emits it fast: it now allows the reciprocal
and approximate functions (not no-NaN or no-infinity: DXIL keeps them). With dxmt/llvm.sh's CreateFDivFMF fix, the
translated divides keep DXIL's flags (B6).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/airconv/dxbc_converter_basicblock.cpp | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/airconv/dxbc_converter_basicblock.cpp b/src/airconv/dxbc_converter_basicblock.cpp
index a289a58..5c338f0 100644
--- a/src/airconv/dxbc_converter_basicblock.cpp
+++ b/src/airconv/dxbc_converter_basicblock.cpp
@@ -237,6 +237,12 @@ IREffect init_input_reg(
         auto rcp_w = ctx.builder.CreateFDiv(
           llvm::ConstantFP::get(ctx.types._float, 1), w_component
         );
+        // MacNeutron (B6): D3D's interpolated w needs no IEEE divide, so a reciprocal, as Metal Shader Converter emits
+        // it (the DXBC path's builder already has fast flags; DXIL's has none). NaN and infinity stay.
+        if (auto *div = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::Instruction>(rcp_w)) {
+          div->setHasAllowReciprocal(true);
+          div->setHasApproxFunc(true);
+        }
         arg = ctx.builder.CreateInsertElement(arg, rcp_w, 3);
       }
       return store_at_vec4_array_masked(
